oes this so early why doesn't the  nfl do this but now everything's so set  in its way in other words the drafts at  a certain time and now they've got tv  and they're making a lot of money off  this thing and the pro days you know  colleges love those things and the  combine now it's going tonight we're  going to make a lot of money off of that  so i don't ever see this thing changing  but it makes so much sense and if you  have the draft earlier you get the  rookies in there earlier and get them in  your program get the playbook in their  hands and you know as kyle shanahan said  you know what you need in free agency  what you didn't address in the draft  instead of coming back in may and june  and going oh my gosh we didn't address  this we've got to try to find this third  and fourth level of free agent to kind  of fill in as we go forward  i think it benefits players generally to  have free agency before the draft but if  the draft comes first it would benefit  players who who happen to be  at the position where maybe there's a  team out there that thought it was going  to address a need in the draft and  failed to do so so would overpay that  specific individual is going to benefit  from being in the right place at the  right time and also one thing that i  would like about draft before free  agency i always get frustrated and i'm  sure the players involved get very  frustrated when there's a quarterback  who signs with a team in march to  ostensibly be the starter and then  that team drafts somebody in round one  or two and that guy's no longer the  starter anymore  i i wouldn't have signed here if i had  known you were gonna draft a quarterback  in round one you flip it around those  veteran quarterbacks know where the  empty seats are they know what they're  getting themselves into when they they  accept that contract  that's a great point mike and we've seen  that a lot over the last couple of years  that veterans sign with those teams  thinking they're going to be there long  term and it turns out that they're a  bridge quarterback in their one year  and i also think that we overthink not  we as but the nfl overthinks  the draft they spend all this time  scouting these players over two years  three years four years however long  they're there they've scouted these  players they know what these players can  do then we go to the combine then we  have pro days then we get into all the  red flags and the concerns and the  wonderlic tests and everything else i  think it takes some of that junk away  and just has you evaluate on what they  did in college what they did in the  combine make them go work out at the  combine let's don't have pro days here's  the combine this is your one opportunity  to work out unless you have a doctor's  note that you're injured let's work out  here and then let's have the draft and  let's go and i think it would make it  much easier on teams than to have to go  through all this garbage time that they  go through in evaluating players to me  it's just much evaluation on players  judge what they did on the field  right i agree with you there the problem  with the combine is unless you're going  to invite 700 players there's always  going to be a guy that's going to do a  pro day workout somewhere because he  didn't get an invitation to the scouting  combine the next thing you know he's  adam thielen so i think that that's part  of the problem part of the challenge  that they would do it but the other  thing and you made this point the nfl  has a perfect off-season schedule now  where it can create revenue  opportunities  big audience  events  with no games being played scouting  combine free agency draft february march  april if you start moving things around  how do you work that calendar and get  the most bang for the buck and i think  that's that's the challenge and so i  think that  the teams may want to do it the media  may like it that way but the league is  going to prefer to keep it the way it is  and i think the players from the  standpoint of the players association  would say we're not changing this we  like having that shotgun approach to  free agency where teams are going to  spend  because they don't know what needs they  ultimately are going to be able to  address in the draft so make them spend  their money before they know what's  behind door number two  yeah i don't ever see this changing even  though a lot of people would like to see  it change just for all those reasons i  think it stays the same and this is the  calendar we have moving forward probably  for eternity in the nfl  we've got over 6 000 votes on the  twitter poll question as to whether  you'd like to see the nfl draft before  free agency so far 55  yes they want the draft and actually  it's ticked up to 56 that's right around  where it's covered one of these things  i've learned about the poll question  the first hundred votes the percentage  generated by those it it's if there's a  hundred thousand votes it's the same  percentage so the sample size doesn't  have to be very big to give us an idea  of where the public stands the public  would like the draft before free agency  i think shereen and i would like it as  well but isn't gonna happen  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights from nbc  sports

